Since
$$
\mathcal F^{-1}[e^{ika}]=\delta(x+a),
\qquad
\mathcal F^{-1}[e^{-ika}]=\delta(x-a),
$$
the <inverse Fourier transforms of phase factors> give
$$
\boxed{
\mathcal F^{-1}[\cos(ka)]
=\frac12\bigl[\delta(x+a)+\delta(x-a)\bigr]
}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{
\mathcal F^{-1}[\sin(ka)]
=\frac1{2i}\bigl[\delta(x+a)-\delta(x-a)\bigr].
}
$$

