Let $G'$ be the set of finite products of commutators. It is closed under products, and $[x,y]^{-1}=[y,x]$, so it is a <subgroup>. Moreover
$$
g^{-1}[x,y]g=[g^{-1}xg,g^{-1}yg],
$$
so conjugation preserves products of commutators and $G'\triangleleft G$. In $G/G'$, one has $xyG'=yxG'$ because $(yx)^{-1}xy=[x,y]$, hence the quotient is abelian. Conversely, if $G/N$ is abelian, every commutator maps to the identity, so $G'\leq N$. This is the universal property of the <commutator subgroup>.

